Title:
Ouroboros — Dev Tunnels “Accidental C2” Post-Exploitation PoC

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
Ouroboros is a Rust proof-of-concept post-exploitation tool that abuses Microsoft Dev Tunnels (used by VSCode Remote) as a covert remote access channel. It authenticates to the Dev Tunnels service using OAuth tokens (GitHub or Azure/Entra ID) and enumerates tunnels associated with a compromised identity, then connects to a selected tunnel to execute utility-style operations. The tool exposes a shell-like interface supporting command execution and filesystem/process actions (read/write/list/rename, spawn processes, kill PIDs, environment/hostname queries). It highlights how authorizing the “Visual Studio Code” OAuth app (including Device Code flow) can bridge from token acquisition/phishing to remote code execution on enrolled dev-tunnel endpoints. It’s primarily useful for red teams and adversary simulation to demonstrate identity-to-endpoint impact via developer tooling, and for defenders to understand and detect misuse of Dev Tunnels and OAuth device code flows.

Technical Focus:
- Microsoft Dev Tunnels protocol and VSCode Remote connectivity
- OAuth2 authentication (GitHub OAuth app, Azure/Entra ID client IDs, Device Code flow)
- Post-compromise tunnel enumeration and remote interaction
- SSH library/protocol deviation handling (russh patch; forwarded-tcpip channel misuse)
- Remote command execution and filesystem/process primitives over tunnel

Use Cases:
- Enumerate Dev Tunnels tied to a compromised GitHub account token
- Device Code phishing → obtain OAuth token → access and operate on dev-tunnel endpoints
- Entra ID token abuse (FOCI-capable client IDs) to reach “Dev Tunnels Service”
- Red team tradecraft: leverage legitimate developer infrastructure as C2-like transport
- Defensive validation: build detections for unusual Dev Tunnels access and OAuth app consent/device-code activity

Keywords:
Microsoft Dev Tunnels, VSCode Remote, Visual Studio Code OAuth, OAuth2, Device Code flow, GitHub OAuth, Entra ID, Azure AD, FOCI, client ID, token abuse, tunnel enumeration, RCE, post-exploitation, russh, SSH protocol, forwarded-tcpip channel, filesystem operations, process control